**My Class → Attendance** opens a dashboard for your class with three ways into the same data. They
are not three reports; they are three zoom levels.

## Which one to open

| Question | View |
| --- | --- |
| "Who was in on Tuesday?" | **Day-wise Summary** |
| "Who is drifting — missing the same day each week?" | **Week-wise Summary** |
| "Who is below the attendance threshold this term?" | **Term Attendance** |
| "I need something to print or hand over" | **Attendance Report** |

## Day-wise Summary

One row per student, one column per day. This is the view for answering a specific question about a
specific date, and for spotting the day the whole class dipped — a wet Monday, a public holiday that
wasn't configured.

## Week-wise Summary

Aggregates the same data by week. Patterns show up here that are invisible day by day: the student
who is reliably out on Fridays, the fortnight someone quietly stopped coming.

If it says **No Data Available**, no attendance has been recorded for the period — which, at the
start of a term, usually just means nobody has marked the register yet. **No Students Found** means
something different: the class has no one enrolled in it.

## Term Attendance

A **Term Overview** with the class's figures, then a **Per-Student Breakdown** with a **Class Avg**
line to compare against. This is the view to use before a parents' evening, and the one that answers
"is this student's attendance actually a problem, or does it just feel like it?"

## Attendance Report

The printable, shareable version. Use it for anything leaving the system.

## A reminder about what "absent" means

None of these views can distinguish a genuine absence from a register that was never completed — an
unmarked student looks identical either way. If a day looks alarming, check whether it was actually
marked before acting on it. See
[Mark Your Class's Attendance](/docs/educonnect-teacher-taking-attendance).

